// An owned call result passed straight into another call has no owner.
//
// **Closed 2026-08-29. This file is now a regression guard, and the ledger it
// asserts is in `expected_leaks` in tests/test_runner.py under this file's own
// name** -- it is still not named `test_*.psm`, because what has to be checked
// is the allocation ledger and not the exit status. The two loops below differ
// by exactly one `let`, which is what makes the comparison mean anything.
//
// Codegen now asks aif_owns_call_result_at_node in **argument position** as well
// as at a binding, and releases the temporary once the enclosing call returns --
// the caller is the last owner, because a Prismio parameter is a borrow. Three
// conditions gate it, and each was a reachable hazard: the callee is a known
// Prismio function (an extern's result is its FFI contract's business), it does
// not return one of its parameters (or the result aliases the temporary), and
// the result carries no pointer (which also covers a returned *view*, whose
// provenance the set intersection cannot see). `spawn` is excluded structurally
// -- it lowers through its own path in generateExpression -- which is required
// rather than incidental, because a spawned task may still be running.
//
// **What the numbers were, and are:**
//
//     before   92 allocated, 52 released, 40 leaked, 0 violation(s)
//     after    92 allocated, 92 released,  0 leaked, 0 violation(s)
//
// Both halves printed 3010520 throughout, so the difference was ownership and
// never arithmetic. The paragraphs below describe the defect as it stood and are
// kept because the reasoning is what the guard is for.
//
// `aif_owns_call_result_at_node` answers "does this frame own what the call
// returned", and codegen asks it at a **binding** -- `VARIABLE_DECL` and
// assignment, in src/ir/stmt.psm. A call result consumed directly as an argument
// is never bound, so nothing asks, and nothing drops it.
//
// Most of the time an automatic region hides this: a callee-allocated value is
// normally reclaimed in bulk by the arena M3 places around the caller's loop.
// When call-site bracketing declines -- `prismio aif --why` says it plainly,
// "0 of 4 call sites lie inside a region -- so no arena would serve it either
// way" -- there is no second owner and the value leaks.
//
// Under `--verify`, built with the compiler of 2026-08-26, this file ran both
// halves and reported **92 allocated, 52 released, 40 leaked** -- every leaked
// allocation belonging to the unbound half. Measured separately at 20 iterations
// each: bound **27/27/0**, unbound **107/27/80**.
//
// **This is not a `spawn` defect**, which is how it was first written up on
// 2026-08-26 before being measured properly. The same file with `spawn`/`join`
// around the calls leaks *identically* -- 107/27/80 either way. `spawn` was
// simply the first thing that happened to be looking.
//
// **The fix needs a guard, not just a drop** -- and the guard turned out to be
// mostly already there. Releasing every owned temporary argument is a
// use-after-free wherever the callee retains it, `list_push` being the obvious
// case; but a callee that retains has already raised `in_container` or
// `site_in_released_field` on the site, and aif_owns_call_result_at_node
// declines on both, so `list_push(l, band(...))` gets no release here. The
// half that was *not* there is a callee handing a parameter back through its
// own return, which is aif_fn_may_return_param -- see
// tests/test_85_passthrough_escape.psm, where the same gap at a *binding*
// segfaulted. A Prismio callee cannot retain a by-value parameter at all: sema
// rejects it with "cannot move out of borrowed value".

import std.io

// An extern with no FFI contract, which is what makes call-site bracketing
// decline -- see the note on `drive` below. `abs` rather than a clock: this file
// is built by the suite on all three platforms, and `clock_gettime_nsec_np` is
// macOS-only, so the Linux and Windows runners could not link it.
extern fn abs(v: Int) -> Int

struct Band {
    seed: Int,
    steps: Int
}

fn simulate(b: Band) -> Int {
    return b.seed + b.steps
}

fn band(f: Int, i: Int, s: Int) -> Band {
    return Band { seed: f * 7919 + i, steps: s }
}

// The samples list and the opaque `abs` calls are not decoration: they are what
// makes call-site bracketing decline, which is what removes the arena that would
// otherwise hide the whole question. An extern with no contract has unknown
// provenance, and that is the property being used -- not anything about `abs`.
fn drive(bound: Bool) -> Int {
    let mut samples: List<Int> = list_new()
    let mut g = 0
    while (g < 20) {
        list_push(samples, 0)
        g = g + 1
    }

    let mut total = 0
    let mut frame = 0
    while (frame < 20) {
        let t0 = abs(frame)
        if (bound) {
            let b0 = band(frame, 0, 32)
            let b1 = band(frame, 1, 32)
            total = total + simulate(b0) + simulate(b1)
        } else {
            total = total + simulate(band(frame, 0, 32)) + simulate(band(frame, 1, 32))
        }
        let t1 = abs(frame + 1)
        list_set(samples, frame, t1 - t0)
        frame = frame + 1
    }
    return total
}

fn main() -> Int {
    print("bound   ")
    println(drive(true))
    print("unbound ")
    println(drive(false))
    return 0
}
